# id: M53-empty-mark-releases-through
# covering-test: TestAnEmptyMarkDoesNotReleaseAnEarlierOne
# package: ./raft/
# expect: killed
# power: n/a -- unreachable in the schedule mix the sweep runs, by argument and by measurement. The defect needs a mark opened and then emptied while an earlier gated message waits, which A5's UNTHROTTLED collector produced constantly: 1 detection in 60 seeds. Throttling the collector to one collection in flight -- a 30x runtime win that made a 10,000-seed exit run viable -- took it to 0 in 300 and then 0 in 3000. That is A2's M34 lesson from the other side: a schedule mix is a claim about reachability, and this change traded some. Killed by TestAnEmptyMarkDoesNotReleaseAnEarlierOne, which builds the sequence by hand
# mutates: a mark that covers nothing releases everything THROUGH it instead of only itself. An empty mark is satisfied because there is nothing to wait for, which is a statement about that mark alone -- it has not earned the monotonicity a durability acknowledgement has. Releasing through it frees messages gated on an earlier mark whose write is still in flight, so a follower advertises a term that is not on its disk (BUG-017)
